# Project Standards

## Technology

- Use the project's installed, supported Nuxt version, Vue 3, TypeScript, and Vuetify 3.
- Use Vue Composition API and `<script setup lang="ts">`.
- Use Vuetify components for all standard UI elements.
- Use Pinia for global client state and TanStack Vue Query for server state.
- Use the existing Django REST API through a centralized, typed API client.
- Do not introduce another UI framework without explicit approval.

## Mobile-first design

- Design for 320px mobile screens first, then tablet and desktop.
- Use responsive layouts and Vuetify's grid and display utilities.
- Use bottom navigation for primary mobile destinations and a navigation drawer on larger screens.
- Use cards or stacked layouts on mobile when desktop tables would overflow.
- Keep forms, dialogs, menus, and action buttons usable on touch screens.
- Never introduce horizontal page overflow.
- Respect safe areas, virtual keyboards, reduced-motion preferences, and accessible touch targets.

## Component architecture

- Reuse shared components, layouts, composables, and design tokens.
- Keep business logic separate from presentation components.
- Use feature-based modules and consistent naming conventions.
- Use Vuetify components before creating custom replacements.
- Avoid giant page components and unnecessary abstractions.
- Build shared components only when reuse provides clear value.

## API and state management

- Never hardcode production API data or fake successful API responses.
- Centralize API calls, authentication, error handling, and pagination.
- Use typed request and response models.
- Handle loading, empty, success, error, and retry states.
- Avoid duplicate requests and unnecessary refetches.
- Never store secrets in frontend code.
- Do not assume hiding a UI action provides authorization.

## Security

- Enforce all permissions on the backend.
- Apply role-aware navigation and frontend route middleware.
- Prevent unauthorized access to protected screens and records.
- Never expose sensitive student, parent, or tutor data unnecessarily.
- Follow secure session or token handling practices appropriate to the backend.

## Quality

- Use strict TypeScript and avoid `any` unless justified.
- Include validation and accessible labels for forms.
- Keep code readable and production-oriented.
- Add tests for important logic and critical user workflows.
- Run linting, type checks, tests, and production builds after meaningful changes.
- Do not disable existing checks merely to make a build pass.

## Workflow

- Inspect existing files and dependencies before editing.
- Propose a small implementation plan before major changes.
- Implement one feature at a time.
- Reuse existing conventions and avoid unrelated refactoring.
- Report changed files, validation performed, known limitations, and next steps.
